<!-- Section 3: sec-what -->
<section id="sec-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is the Altitude of a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>An altitude is a perpendicular segment drawn from one vertex of a triangle to the line containing the opposite side. In a right triangle, the most useful altitude starts at the right-angle vertex and meets the hypotenuse at a right angle. The point where it lands is called the foot of the altitude, and the hypotenuse is the natural base for this base-height pair.</p>
  <p>Every triangle has three altitudes, and a right triangle is no exception. Two of them coincide with the legs: if you take one leg as the base, the other leg is already perpendicular to it, so it serves as the altitude. The third altitude runs from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse and lies inside the triangle. This page calculates that third one, the only altitude that requires real computation.</p>
  <p>Because it meets the hypotenuse at a right angle, this altitude divides the original triangle into two smaller right triangles. Those two triangles are similar to each other and to the original, which is why the altitude creates the neat geometric-mean relationships explored later in this guide.</p>
</section>

