  <p>Each side has a standard label: AC = a, BC = b, AB = c, and the altitude CD = h. The altitude splits the hypotenuse into two projections, AD = p and DB = q, so c = p + q. Notice that p lies next to leg a and q lies next to leg b, a pattern that matters for the similarity proof later.</p>
  <p>Reading the diagram from left to right shows the whole story: a right triangle, an altitude dropping to the hypotenuse, two projection segments, and the original triangle split into two smaller right triangles, ACD and CBD, that share the altitude as a common side.</p>
