  <h2>Right Triangle Altitude Formulas Explained</h2>
  <p>Four formulas find the same altitude, and each starts from different known measurements. The live calculator implements the first two directly; the other two are manual methods you can use when your measurements are incomplete or when you want to verify a result another way.</p>
  <p>The sides formula <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]')} /> needs both legs and the hypotenuse. The projections formula <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{pq}]]')} /> needs only the two hypotenuse segments, and it is equivalent to the altitude theorem <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=pq]]')} />. When you know the legs but not the hypotenuse, substitute <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]]')} /> to get <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{ab}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}]]')} />. When you know the area A and the hypotenuse c, use <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{2A}{c}]]')} />, which comes straight from the area formula with the hypotenuse as the base.</p>
  <p>Never treat these as four calculator modes. The calculator keeps exactly two modes: Altitude to Hypotenuse and Altitude Squared. The remaining formulas are helpers for manual calculation, for deriving a missing measurement before entering the calculator, or for double-checking an answer the calculator produced.</p>
