<!-- Section 7: sec-method1 -->
<section id="sec-method1" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Method 1: Find Right Triangle Altitude From Side Lengths</h2>
  <p>When all three side lengths are known, the altitude comes from a single division. The formula <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]')} /> multiplies the two legs and divides by the hypotenuse, and each symbol has a fixed meaning: a and b are the perpendicular legs, c is the hypotenuse, and h is the altitude to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>The classic 3-4-5 triangle shows every step. Given a = 3 cm, b = 4 cm, and c = 5 cm, substitute into the formula:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{(3 \\times 4)}{5}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Multiply the legs first: 3 × 4 = 12. Then divide by the hypotenuse:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{12}{5}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result is h = 2.4 cm. Notice that 2.4 is shorter than both legs, which always happens for the altitude to the hypotenuse, and the proof section later explains why the division by c is geometrically natural.</p>
  <p>Use this method whenever the three sides are known or can be measured directly. For a dedicated tool that handles only side inputs, try the <a href="/right-triangle-altitude-from-sides-calculator/">altitude-from-sides calculator</a>, which walks through the same formula in more depth.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-sides-345.svg')} alt="Right triangle with legs 3 and 4, hypotenuse 5, altitude 2.4 drawn perpendicular to the hypotenuse" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">A 3-4-5 triangle: altitude h equals (3 x 4) / 5 = 2.4.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-method2 -->
<section id="sec-method2" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Method 2: Find Altitude From Hypotenuse Projections</h2>
  <p>When the hypotenuse is already split into its two projections, the altitude theorem gives a shortcut. The theorem states <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=pq]]')} />, so the altitude is the square root of the product of the two projections:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{pq}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Take p = 9 cm and q = 16 cm. Square the relationship first:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h^2=9 \\times 16]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Multiply: 9 × 16 = 144, so <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=144]]')} />. Now take the square root:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{144}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result is h = 12 cm. This says the altitude is the geometric mean of the two projections: it sits between them in size, closer to their average than to either extreme, a meaning explained more fully in the geometric-mean section.</p>
  <p>Remember that p and q are segments of the hypotenuse, not the triangle's legs. A common mistake is entering leg lengths as projections, which produces a wrong answer. For a tool built around this method alone, see the <a href="/right-triangle-altitude-from-projections-calculator/">altitude-from-projections calculator</a>.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-projections.svg')} alt="Right triangle with altitude h splitting the hypotenuse into projections p = 9 and q = 16, altitude h = 12" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The altitude is the geometric mean of the projections: sqrt(9 x 16) = 12.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-two-sides -->
<section id="sec-two-sides" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find Altitude When Only Two Sides Are Known</h2>
  <p>Sometimes the third side is missing, but the Pythagorean theorem recovers it in one step. The recovery method depends on which two sides you know, so handle each case separately.</p>
  <h3>Case 1: Both Legs Are Known</h3>
  <p>Given a = 6 cm and b = 8 cm, find the hypotenuse first with <span set:html={rich('[[c=\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c=\\sqrt{6^2+8^2}=\\sqrt{36+64}=\\sqrt{100}=10]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Now all three sides are known, so apply the sides formula: h = (6 × 8) / 10 = 48 / 10 = 4.8 cm. The missing hypotenuse was the only obstacle, and the altitude follows directly.</p>
  <h3>Case 2: One Leg and the Hypotenuse Are Known</h3>
  <p>Given a = 5 cm and c = 13 cm, solve for the missing leg with <span set:html={rich('[[b=\\sqrt{c^2-a^2}]]')} />:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b=\\sqrt{13^2-5^2}=\\sqrt{169-25}=\\sqrt{144}=12]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With b = 12 cm recovered, the altitude is h = (5 × 12) / 13 = 60/13 cm ≈ 4.615 cm. These are manual derivations for finding the required measurements first; the calculator itself asks for complete inputs, so do this groundwork on paper before entering values.</p>
  <p>If you frequently need the missing side itself, the <a href="/right-triangle-side-calculator/">side calculator</a> finds it directly before you move on to the altitude.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: sec-why -->
<section id="sec-why" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Right Triangle Altitude Formula Work?</h2>
  <p>The formula works because one triangle can be measured with two different base-height pairs, and the area stays the same either way. This area argument is the shortest honest proof of <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]')} />.</p>
  <p>First, use the legs as the base-height pair. Since the legs are perpendicular, the area is simply half their product:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A=\\frac{ab}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Now use the hypotenuse as the base and the altitude as the height. The same triangle has the same area:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A=\\frac{ch}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Two expressions for one area, so set them equal:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{ab}{2}=\\frac{ch}{2}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Multiply both sides by 2 to clear the fractions, giving ab = ch. Divide both sides by c:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Every step is reversible arithmetic, so the division by the hypotenuse is not a trick; it is the algebraic price of switching from the leg base-height pair to the hypotenuse base-height pair while the area stays fixed.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-area-comparison.svg')} alt="The same right triangle drawn twice: first with base a and height b, then with base c and height h" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Same triangle, two base-height pairs: area equals ab/2 and ch/2, so h equals ab/c.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-proof -->
<section id="sec-proof" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Proof of the Altitude Theorem Using Similar Triangles</h2>
  <p>The projection formula <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{pq}]]')} /> comes from similarity. Keep the consistent labeling: triangle ABC is right-angled at C, D lies on AB, CD is perpendicular to AB, AD = p, DB = q, and CD = h.</p>
  <p>The altitude splits ABC into two smaller right triangles, ACD and CBD. Angle at A is shared between ABC and ACD, so those two are similar; likewise angle at B is shared between ABC and CBD. Since both smaller triangles are similar to the original, they are similar to each other: triangle ACD is similar to triangle CBD.</p>
  <p>Write the similarity as a proportion of corresponding sides. The short leg of ACD is p and the long leg is h; in CBD the short leg is h and the long leg is q. Matching them gives:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{p}{h}=\\frac{h}{q}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Cross-multiply: h × h = p × q, so h² = pq. Take the square root:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\sqrt{pq}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Only the positive root is used because side lengths are positive. A negative root has no geometric meaning here. The key insight is that the altitude is the mean proportional between the two hypotenuse segments, which is exactly what a geometric mean is.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-similar-triangles.svg')} alt="Original right triangle ABC with altitude CD, beside the two smaller similar triangles ACD and CBD with corresponding sides labeled" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Triangles ACD and CBD are similar: p/h = h/q, so h squared equals p times q.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Right Triangle Altitude</h2>
  <p>Seven examples cover both calculator modes, unit conversion, and decimal measurements. Each shows the given values, the chosen formula, the substitution, and the final answer.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Classic 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given a = 3 cm, b = 4 cm, c = 5 cm. Use <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]')} />: h = (3 × 4) / 5 = 12 / 5 = 2.4 cm. The altitude is shorter than either leg, as always.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: 5-12-13 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given a = 5 m, b = 12 m, c = 13 m. Use <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]')} />: h = (5 × 12) / 13 = 60/13 m ≈ 4.615 m. Keep the fraction until the last step for a clean decimal.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: 8-15-17 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given a = 8 cm, b = 15 cm, c = 17 cm. Use <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]')} />: h = (8 × 15) / 17 = 120/17 cm ≈ 7.059 cm. Larger legs still give an altitude smaller than both.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Equal Hypotenuse Projections</h3>
  <p>Given p = 5 cm and q = 5 cm. Use <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{pq}]]')} />: h = √(5 × 5) = √25 = 5 cm. Equal projections mean the altitude is as tall as possible for that hypotenuse, and the triangle is an isosceles right triangle: each leg is 5√2 cm, roughly 7.07 cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Unequal Hypotenuse Projections</h3>
  <p>Given p = 4 cm and q = 25 cm. Use <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{pq}]]')} />: h = √(4 × 25) = √100 = 10 cm. Notice how the altitude of 10 sits between the two projections, 4 and 25, which is the hallmark of the geometric mean.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Decimal Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given a = 1.5 m, b = 2 m, c = 2.5 m. Use <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{ab}{c}]]')} />: h = (1.5 × 2) / 2.5 = 3 / 2.5 = 1.2 m. Decimals work exactly like whole numbers as long as every value uses the same unit.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Different Measurement Units</h3>
  <p>Given a = 30 cm, b = 0.4 m, c = 0.5 m. Convert everything to centimeters first: b = 40 cm, c = 50 cm. Then h = (30 × 40) / 50 = 1200 / 50 = 24 cm. Converting before substituting avoids the most common unit error.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-area -->
<section id="sec-area" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Altitude and Triangle Area</h2>
  <p>The area and the altitude determine each other once the hypotenuse is known. With the hypotenuse as the base, area A = ch/2, so solving for the altitude gives <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{2A}{c}]]')} />. This is a genuinely different route: it skips the legs entirely.</p>
  <p>Suppose a problem states the area A = 54 cm² and the hypotenuse c = 15 cm. Substitute:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h=\\frac{2 \\times 54}{15}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Multiply the numerator: 2 × 54 = 108. Then divide: h = 108 / 15 = 7.2 cm. The altitude follows with no knowledge of the individual legs.</p>
  <p>This route is useful when the area is given directly, for example in word problems about land or material. It also serves as a verification tool: compute the altitude from the sides, then confirm that 2A/c gives the same value. The <a href="/right-triangle-area-calculator/">area calculator</a> finds the area from whatever measurements a problem provides.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-geomean -->
<section id="sec-geomean" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Geometric Mean and Hypotenuse Projections</h2>
  <p>The altitude theorem says the altitude is the geometric mean of the two projections, and the geometric mean is a specific kind of average. Take p = 4 and q = 9. Their geometric mean is √(4 × 9) = √36 = 6, so a triangle with those projections has altitude 6.</p>
  <p>Compare that with the ordinary arithmetic mean: (4 + 9) / 2 = 6.5. The two averages differ, and only 6 is the correct altitude. The arithmetic mean averages by adding, while the geometric mean averages by multiplying, which suits lengths that scale together rather than add.</p>
  <p>Geometrically, the mean-proportional relationship h² = pq means the altitude relates to each projection the same way the projections relate to each other. This symmetry is what makes the two smaller triangles similar, and it is why the altitude always lies between the two projections in size, never equal to the arithmetic mean unless p equals q.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Important Properties of Right Triangle Altitude</h2>
  <p>Seven properties describe the altitude to the hypotenuse. Together they give a reliable intuition for what altitude values are possible and how they behave.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The altitude to the hypotenuse is perpendicular to it, so it is the shortest distance from the right-angle vertex to the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>The altitude divides the triangle into two smaller triangles, each similar to the original and to each other.</li>
    <li>The altitude is shorter than both legs; neither leg can be the shortest path to the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>The altitude is shorter than the hypotenuse itself, since a perpendicular segment is the shortest route.</li>
    <li>The altitude is the geometric mean of the two hypotenuse projections, <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\sqrt{pq}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>For a fixed hypotenuse, the altitude is greatest when the two projections are equal, which happens when the two legs are equal.</li>
    <li>Scaling every side length by the same factor scales the altitude by that factor; double the triangle, double the altitude.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The sixth property has a precise bound: the altitude can never exceed half the hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[h \\leq \\frac{c}{2}]]')} />, with equality only when the legs are equal. A 45-45-90 triangle with hypotenuse 10 has altitude exactly 5, the maximum; a 6-8-10 triangle with the same hypotenuse reaches only 4.8, as the diagram shows.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('altitude-equal-vs-unequal.svg')} alt="Two right triangles sharing a hypotenuse of 10: a 45-45-90 triangle with altitude 5 and a 6-8-10 style triangle with altitude 4" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Same hypotenuse 10: equal projections give the tallest altitude, 5.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Altitude Answer Is Correct</h2>
  <p>Three independent checks catch calculation errors. Use at least one of them whenever an altitude answer matters.</p>
  <h3>Check 1: Triangle Area</h3>
  <p>Compare ab/2 with ch/2 using your altitude. For the 3-4-5 triangle with h = 2.4: ab/2 = (3 × 4)/2 = 6, and ch/2 = (5 × 2.4)/2 = 6. Matching areas confirm the altitude fits the sides.</p>
  <h3>Check 2: Geometric Mean</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[h^2=pq]]')} /> when the projections are known. With p = 9 and q = 16: h² = 12² = 144, and pq = 9 × 16 = 144. Equality means the altitude is consistent with how the hypotenuse splits.</p>
  <h3>Check 3: Pythagorean Theorem</h3>
  <p>Confirm <span set:html={rich('[[a^2+b^2=c^2]]')} /> for the given sides: 9 + 16 = 25 in the 3-4-5 case. This check validates the side lengths, not the altitude directly, but a wrong side entry is the most common reason an altitude answer fails the other two checks.</p>
  <p>Run every check in consistent units. Mixing centimeters with meters breaks the area check silently, so convert first and verify second.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Altitude</h2>
  <p>Ten errors show up again and again. Each one is easy to spot once you know its signature.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Confusing altitude with a leg. The altitude meets the hypotenuse at a right angle; a leg meets the other leg at the right angle. In a 3-4-5 triangle, h = 2.4, not 3 or 4.</li>
    <li>Choosing the wrong calculator mode. Entering legs and a hypotenuse into the Altitude Squared mode, which expects projections, produces nonsense. Match the mode to the measurements.</li>
    <li>Confusing projections with legs. Entering a = 3 and b = 4 as p and q computes √(12) ≈ 3.46, which is not the altitude. Projections are hypotenuse segments.</li>
    <li>Using the wrong hypotenuse. Dividing ab by a leg instead of the hypotenuse, e.g. 12/4 = 3, overstates the altitude. The divisor must be the longest side, c.</li>
    <li>Forgetting the square root in the projection formula. Stopping at h² = 144 gives 144 as the altitude; the real answer is h = 12.</li>
    <li>Mixing centimeters and meters. With a = 30 cm and c = 0.5 m, substituting 0.5 directly gives a wrong altitude. Convert first: 50 cm.</li>
    <li>Entering invalid side lengths. Sides like 2, 3, 10 do not satisfy the Pythagorean theorem, so no right triangle exists and no altitude is defined.</li>
    <li>Rounding too early. Rounding 60/13 to 4.6 before a further step carries the error forward. Keep the fraction and round only the final answer.</li>
    <li>Treating h² as the final altitude. If a calculation ends with h² = 36, the altitude is 6, not 36.</li>
    <li>Applying right-triangle formulas to an arbitrary triangle. The formulas here need a right angle; for other triangles the altitude relationships differ.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-World Applications of Right Triangle Altitude</h2>
  <p>Altitude calculations support any work built on right-triangle geometry. In geometry education, the altitude theorem gives students a first taste of similarity proofs and geometric means. Technical drawing and geometric design use the altitude to position perpendiculars when laying out triangular panels or bracket shapes.</p>
  <p>Surveying geometry uses right-triangle relations when mapping plots with perpendicular offsets, and construction planning models use them when a right-triangular frame must meet a wall at a right angle. Area calculations run the other way: from a known area and hypotenuse, <span set:html={rich('[[h=\\frac{2A}{c}]]')} /> recovers the altitude without measuring the legs.</p>
  <p>These are hypothetical geometric sketches, not engineering procedures. Real surveying and structural work requires suitable measurements, design assumptions, and professional standards; the altitude theorem describes the geometry, it never replaces professional methods.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Answers</h2>
  <p>Five problems use values not seen in the main examples. Solve each, then check the answers below.</p>
  <h3>Problem 1: Beginner Side-Based</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has legs a = 9 cm and b = 40 cm with hypotenuse c = 41 cm. Find the altitude to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Problem 2: Beginner Projection-Based</h3>
  <p>The altitude of a right triangle splits the hypotenuse into p = 16 m and q = 36 m. Find the altitude.</p>
  <h3>Problem 3: Decimal Measurements</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has a = 0.6 m, b = 0.8 m, c = 1.0 m. Find the altitude to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Problem 4: Missing Side</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has leg a = 7 in and hypotenuse c = 25 in. Find the altitude to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Problem 5: Garden Bed Area</h3>
  <p>A right-triangle garden bed has area A = 96 m² and hypotenuse 20 m. Find the altitude to the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Practice Problem Answers</h3>
  <p>Problem 1: h = (9 × 40) / 41 = 360/41 cm ≈ 8.780 cm. First confirm the sides: 9² + 40² = 81 + 1600 = 1681 = 41², so the triangle is valid.</p>
  <p>Problem 2: h = √(16 × 36) = √576 = 24 m. The altitude sits between the two projections, as the geometric mean must.</p>
  <p>Problem 3: h = (0.6 × 0.8) / 1.0 = 0.48 / 1.0 = 0.48 m. Decimals need the same treatment as whole numbers.</p>
  <p>Problem 4: Recover the missing leg: b = √(25² − 7²) = √(625 − 49) = √576 = 24 in. Then h = (7 × 24) / 25 = 168/25 in ≈ 6.72 in.</p>
  <p>Problem 5: Use the area route: h = 2A/c = (2 × 96) / 20 = 192 / 20 = 9.6 m. No leg measurements were needed.</p>
</section>

